\begin_layout Title
The Effect of Budgets on Innovation: A Look At The Video Game Indusy
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Date
March 4, 2014
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The video game industry corresponds to the economic sector involved with
 the development, marketing, and sales of video games.
 Over the years, the video game industry has grow to be one of the largest
 entertainment sectors in the world.
 In fact, the global video game market was valued at $65 billion in June
 2011.
 The United States has the largest market in terms of revenue and employees,
 with the industry as a whole being worth $10.3 billion.
 As a result, the video game industry has a major impact on the economy
 through the sales of games and major systems.
 However, despite increasing valuation of the market, the video game industry
 faces many internal problems.
 These problems are are so significant that the entire video game industry
 is tinkering on the verge of collapse.
 Once of the main problems faced by the video game industry today is the
 rapid escalation of video game budgets.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Now, in the late 1980s and early 1990s, the development costs of making
 a game were minimal.
 a company only needed a small team of programmers to create a game, which
 would only take a few months to create.
 As a result, companies could release several games a year, and large profits
 could be made on a single game.
 However, as computing and graphics power has increased, the size of development
 teams have grown to address the many graphical and programming complexities.
 Unlike other entertainment industries, such as music, modern video games
 require a large amount of labor and capital.
 Consequently, budgets to develop a single video game have risen to the
 millions of dollars due to high labor costs.
 Also, most games require two to three years of development, which puts
 additional strain on budgets.
 In the early 1990s, the average game could be made for $800,000.
 Today, the average games costs 30 times as much, with the number swelling
 to about $28 million.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Moreover, since a video game can be considered a durable good, studios must
 compete with an active second-hand market.
 What the studios sell today will become substitutes to their own products
 in the future.
 In addition, there is a large indie video game market on the internet,
 which enables consumers to purchase games for under $10.
 For video game studios to overcome the problems caused by the second-hand
 and indie markets, they must be willing to innovate.
 Studios should develop new games with updated gameplay, quality graphics,
 and unique storylines.
 Yet, the budgetary conerns previously addressed hinders studios from doing
 this.
 If the studios tried to innovate, then they could serious monetary troubles,
 or even bankrupcy, if the game happened to fail.
 Consequently, many studios choose to release sequels to popular titles
 instead of trying to create a truly unique game.
 This is why the market is saturated with first-person shooters, popular
 sports titles, and rehashings of previously original titles.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Thus, my proposal is to measure the effect rising video game budgets have
 had on the market shares for sequels.
 Given the explanation above, I can expect that rising budgets have increased
 the market shares for sequels
\end_layout

